.TH DWM 1 dwm\-VERSION
.SH NAME
dwm \- dynamic window manager (DrMeosch's build, based on Luke Smith's build)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dwm
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
dwm is a dynamic window manager for X.
.P
dwm "orders" windows based on recency and primacy, while dwm layouts may
change, the most recent "master" window is shown in the most prominent
position. There are bindings for cycling through and promoting windows to the
master position.
.P
Windows are grouped by tags. Each window can be tagged with one or multiple
tags. Selecting certain tags displays all windows with these tags.
.P
Each screen contains a small status bar which displays all available tags, the
layout, the title of the focused window, and the text read from the root window
name property, if the screen is focused. A floating window is indicated with an
empty square and a maximised floating window is indicated with a filled square
before the windows title.  The selected tags are indicated with a different
color. The tags of the focused window are indicated with a filled square in the
top left corner.  The tags which are applied to one or more windows are
indicated with an empty square in the top left corner.
.P
dwm draws a small border around windows to indicate the focus state.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
prints version information to stderr, then exits.
.SH USAGE
.SS Status bar
.TP
.B X root window name
is read and displayed in the status text area. It can be set with the
.BR xsetroot (1)
command.
.IP
Text can be colored with status codes, each written as
.BI ^ code ^ :
.RS
.TP
.BI c #rrggbb
foreground color
.TP
.BI b #rrggbb
background color
.TP
.BI C n
foreground from Xresources dwm.color<n> (n from 0 to 15)
.TP
.BI B n
background from Xresources dwm.color<n>
.TP
.B d
back to the status bar colors
.TP
.BI r x , y , w , h
filled rectangle in the foreground color, x pixels from the current position;
it does not move the position
.TP
.BI f n
move the position n pixels to the right
.RE
.IP
Numbers have one to four digits. dwm.color<n> falls back to a built\-in palette
unless set to exactly #rrggbb. A lone ^, an incomplete or unknown code, or a
value out of range is drawn as plain text. Codes take no space, and clicks on
the status text still reach the correct block. For example:
.B ^c#ff5555^CPU 12%^d^
.TP
.B Left click
click on a tag label to display all windows with that tag, click on the layout
label toggles between tiled and floating layout.
.TP
.B Right click
click on a tag label adds/removes all windows with that tag to/from the view.
.TP
.B Super\-Left click
click on a tag label applies that tag to the focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-Right click
click on a tag label adds/removes that tag to/from the focused window.
.SS Keyboard commands
.TP
.B Super\-Return
Start terminal,
.BR st(1).
.TP
.B Super\-d
Spawn
.BR dmenu(1)
for launching other programs.
.TP
.B Super\-grave
Spawn
.BR dmenuunicode(1)
for selecting emoji.
.TP
.B Super\-minus/plus, Super\-Shift\-minus/plus
Decrease/increase volume by 5 and 15 respectively.
.TP
.B Super\-b
Toggles bar on and off.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-b
Shows or hides the system monitor section of the bar (see
.BR "System monitor" ).
.TP
.B Super\-q
Close focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-t/T
Sets tiled/bstack layouts.
.TP
.B Super\-f
Toggle fullscreen window.
.TP
.B Super\-F
Toggle floating layout.
.TP
.B Super\-y/Y
Sets Fibonacci spiral/dwinde layouts.
.TP
.B Super\-u/U
Sets centered master layout.
.TP
.B Super\-i/I
Sets centered master or floating master layouts.
.TP
.B Super\-space
Zooms/cycles focused window to/from master area.
.TP
.B Super\-j/k
Focus next/previous window.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-j/k
Move selected window down/up in stack.
.TP
.B Super\-o/O
Increase/decrease number of windows in master area.
.TP
.B Super\-l
Increase master area size.
.TP
.B Super\-h
Decrease master area size.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-space
Toggle focused window between tiled and floating state.
.TP
.B Super\-Tab
Toggles to the previously selected tags.
.TP
.B Super\-g
Moves to the previous tag.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-g
Moves selected window to the previous tag.
.TP
.B Super\-;
Moves to the next tag.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-;
Moves selected window to the next tag.
.TP
.B Super\-PageUp
Moves to the previous tag.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-PageUp
Moves selected window to the previous tag.
.TP
.B Super\-Pagedown
Moves to the next tag.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-PageDown
Moves selected window to the next tag.
.TP
.B Super\-a
Toggle gaps.
.TP
.B Super\-z
Increase gaps between windows.
.TP
.B Super\-x
Decrease gaps between windows.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-[1..n]
Apply nth tag to focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-0
Apply all tags to focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-Control\-Shift\-[1..n]
Add/remove nth tag to/from focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-[1..n]
View all windows with nth tag.
.TP
.B Super\-0
View all windows with any tag.
.TP
.B Super\-Control\-[1..n]
Add/remove all windows with nth tag to/from the view.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-q
Quit dwm.
.TP
.B Mod1\-Control\-Shift\-q
Menu to refresh/quit/reboot/shutdown.
.SS Mouse commands
.TP
.B Super\-Left click
Move focused window while dragging. Tiled windows will be toggled to the floating state.
.TP
.B Super\-Middle click
Toggles focused window between floating and tiled state.
.TP
.B Super\-Right click
Resize focused window while dragging. Tiled windows will be toggled to the floating state.
.SS System monitor
An optional section in the middle of the bar shows the CPU
usage, a small graph of its recent samples, the CPU temperature and the
speed of every fan where the machine has them, and the GPU usage with a graph
of its own where the machine has a GPU whose usage can be read, as
.B "CPU  42%"
followed by the graph,
.B "55\(deC  2400/2350rpm  GPU   7%"
and the GPU graph.
A graph has a bar for each of the last 16 samples, the newest on the right,
rising from the baseline of the text to about the height of its digits; an
idle sample is a line, and a sample not taken yet is left empty. The width of
each graph is
.B sysmongraph
in
.I config.h
(32 pixels: 2 for each bar; 0 leaves the graphs out).
A temperature, fan or GPU the machine does not report is not shown at all.
It is off by default. Super\-Shift\-b toggles it, and the Xresources
.B dwm.showsysmon
(0 or 1) sets it at start\-up and on every SIGUSR2 reload; a key press is kept
across a reload unless that resource is set.
.PP
dwm does not measure anything. The separate
.B dwmsysmon
program, which dwm starts whenever the section is shown (at start\-up, by
Super\-Shift\-b or by a reload) and which exits as soon as the section is
hidden, reads
.I /proc/stat
and the sensors below
.IR /sys ,
and publishes the latest values as a short string in the root window property
.B _DWM_SYSMON
(for example
.BR "cpu=42 temp=55 fan=2400,2350 cpuhist=40,38,42 gpu=7 gpuhist=5,7" ).
dwm only draws what it last received. The section shows
.B CPU \-
when there is no CPU value: the sampler is not running, hung or cannot read its data,
the property is malformed (anything but a string of at most 256 bytes with
.B cpu=
and an integer from 0 to 100), or no new value arrived for
.B dwm.sysmonstale
milliseconds (default three times the sampling interval, 12000; it must stay above
that interval).
The temperature
.RB ( temp= ,
0 to 150 degrees Celsius), the fans
.RB ( fan= ,
up to four comma separated speeds from 0 to 30000 RPM), the graph's samples
.RB ( cpuhist= ,
up to 16 comma separated CPU usages from 0 to 100, oldest first), the GPU usage
.RB ( gpu= ,
0 to 100) and its graph's samples
.RB ( gpuhist= ,
as for the CPU) are left out
in the same cases, and each one also when only it is malformed or given twice;
a graph is then empty, and the GPU usage shows as
.B GPU \-
beside a graph that has samples. A
missing, crashed or stuck sampler never blocks or crashes dwm, and dwm has no timer
while there is no value to expire. The section is centred horizontally in the bar,
whatever the tags and layout symbol on the left and the status text on the right take,
and as wide as the fields it has and the graph can get, so it does not move while
their values change.
It never overlaps them: when the centre is short of room, because a long status
text reaches it or the title would be left with less than a bar height, the
graphs give up their oldest bars first, both the same, keeping the section as far
from the status as a graph is from its usage. Only when the section does not fit
even without its graphs is it left out, until there is room again.
.PP
.B dwmsysmon
takes no options and samples the CPU every 4000 ms. The interval is
.B SYSMONINTERVAL
in
.I config.h
(250 to 60000 ms); dwm passes it to the sampler in
.BR _DWM_SYSMON_ENABLED ,
and the default staleness limit follows it. The sampler keeps its last 16
CPU and GPU usage samples for the graphs, and starts over after a reading that fails.
A dwm from before the graph shows
.B CPU \-
for a sampler that sends the samples until it is restarted, and so does a dwm
from before the GPU usage for a payload longer than 128 bytes, which only one
with a GPU can be. The temperature is that of the
.BR coretemp ,
.BR k10temp ,
.B zenpower
or
.B cpu_thermal
hwmon chip, in that order (the package or die sensor, and the hottest package
when there are several), else of the
.BR x86_pkg_temp ,
.B cpu-thermal
or
.B cpu_thermal
thermal zone. The fans are every
.B fan*_input
of the hwmon chips other than GPUs, up to four; a chip that is slow to read
.RB ( dell_smm ,
which reports the same fans as
.B dell_ddv )
is only used when no other chip has fans. Sensors are read with a CPU sample at most every 6 seconds,
and less often, up to once a minute, when a read takes long; a missing, failing
or malformed sensor is left out and looked for again at most once a minute.
.PP
The GPU usage is that of the busiest of the first four
.B card*
devices below
.I /sys/class/drm
with a usage that needs no privileges: the
.B gpu_busy_percent
of an
.B amdgpu
device, or for an Intel
.RB ( i915 )
GPU the share of its highest frequency
.RB ( gt_max_freq_mhz )
that it runs at
.RB ( gt_act_freq_mhz ),
which only roughly follows how busy it is. NVIDIA's driver offers no such
source, and neither does a GPU without a
.B power/runtime_status
of its device, so they are not shown. Reading the usage of a GPU that is
runtime\-suspended would wake it up, and a read of one that is awake keeps it
awake for its
.BR power/autosuspend_delay_ms .
So the sampler reads a GPU only while its
.B runtime_status
is
.B active
or
.BR unsupported ,
counts it as idle (0%) without reading it while it is
.B suspended
or
.BR suspending ,
has no value for it otherwise or when the read fails (as with EBUSY, from a GPU
that has just suspended), and reads the GPUs with a CPU sample, but only once
their longest autosuspend delay and a second have passed since the last read.
Missing GPUs are looked for again at most once a minute. When dwm hides the
section (property
.BR _DWM_SYSMON_ENABLED ,
set by dwm) the sampler removes its value and exits, so no sampler runs while
the section is off, which it is by default; one started while it is hidden
exits at once. dwm never signals or waits for the sampler. The sampler also
removes its value when it exits on SIGTERM, SIGINT or SIGHUP, and it exits when
the X connection closes.
.PP
Each X display has one sampler. A newly started
.B dwmsysmon
takes over that display and the previous one, even if it is stuck reading a
sensor and missed that the section was hidden, retires itself as soon as it
looks at its events; no process is ever
signalled by name, so samplers of other X sessions of the same user keep
running. The sampler also exits when dwm exits or dies while the X server
keeps running, and removes its value then.
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
dwm is customized by creating a custom config.h and (re)compiling the source
code. This keeps it fast, secure and simple.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM - 15
Cleanly terminate the dwm process.
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
Reload colors and border width from Xresources without changing the selected tags.
Merge all four values for the chosen theme before requesting the reload.
dwm.color0 and dwm.color4 reset to their compiled defaults before each read,
the status palette dwm.color0 to dwm.color15 does the same,
and the selected border and width reset to their fallbacks, so missing theme
keys cannot retain values from the previous theme. The status text is
drawn through the same palette on every monitor. In the light palette, the
status area uses the selected pale-pink background; normal bar areas use a
lighter pink, and selected tags and titles use the selected background. In the
dark palette, all bar backgrounds follow dwm.color0 and dwm.color4 supplies
text only. The bar requests 92% opacity in the light palette through
_NET_WM_WINDOW_OPACITY, which requires an existing X11 compositor honoring
that property on override-redirect windows. The dark palette removes that
property and restores the compositor's default handling.
.PP
For the light palette:
.nf
printf '%s\en' \\
  'dwm.color0: #f8f8f2' \\
  'dwm.color4: #555b6e' \\
  'dwm.borderpx: 2' \\
  'dwm.selbordercolor: #b84f7b' | xrdb -merge
pkill -USR2 -x dwm
.fi
.PP
For the dark palette:
.nf
printf '%s\en' \\
  'dwm.color0: #282a36' \\
  'dwm.color4: #ff79c6' \\
  'dwm.borderpx: 1' \\
  'dwm.selbordercolor: #b84f7b' | xrdb -merge
pkill -USR2 -x dwm
.fi
.PP
The focused client border uses dwm.selbordercolor. Both palettes use the same
muted pink accent. If dwm.selbordercolor is absent, the selected border
uses the configured fallback #ffb86c. dwm.color8 does not control it.
.PP
The light palette also sets existing client borders to two pixels; the dark
palette uses one pixel. If dwm.borderpx is missing or invalid, dwm uses the
configured fallback, which is two pixels by default.
.TP
.B dwm.borderpx
Sets the window border width in pixels. Use a positive decimal integer up to
65535. For example, set this to 1 for a dark theme or 2 for a light theme.
Existing windows receive the new width after a SIGUSR2 reload. If the resource
is missing, invalid, or non-positive, dwm uses the configured fallback, which
is 2 pixels by default.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Reserved for the dwmblocks notification protocol; it does not request a dwm reload.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dmenu (1),
.BR st (1)
.SH ISSUES
Java applications which use the XToolkit/XAWT backend may draw grey windows
only. The XToolkit/XAWT backend breaks ICCCM-compliance in recent JDK 1.5 and early
JDK 1.6 versions, because it assumes a reparenting window manager. Possible workarounds
are using JDK 1.4 (which doesn't contain the XToolkit/XAWT backend) or setting the
environment variable
.BR AWT_TOOLKIT=MToolkit
(to use the older Motif backend instead) or running
.B xprop -root -f _NET_WM_NAME 32a -set _NET_WM_NAME LG3D
or
.B wmname LG3D
(to pretend that a non-reparenting window manager is running that the
XToolkit/XAWT backend can recognize) or when using OpenJDK setting the environment variable
.BR _JAVA_AWT_WM_NONREPARENTING=1 .
